 [17592186420841 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186420819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420841 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "660640e3-e8ef-509a-a758-3c65a1428537" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420848 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420848 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43071" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420848 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420848 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420848 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37f105cd-9dd0-500b-bb29-7f9f80a1a14c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420848 #edb/ref 1074 "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6-2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420849 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420849 #edb/ref 1069 "q-88281" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420849 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420849 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(A).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420849 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "38d108f6-377b-5951-bc6b-4ec43d8b652b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420849 #edb/ref 1074 "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 2)\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-4)) \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =-72.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428341 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428341 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: linear=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/linear-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1774->148. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428341 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186050765 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186435742 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435742 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435742 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435742 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435742 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435742 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435742 #edb/ref 1045 "Further Properties of Determinants" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435742 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186435774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435742 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435742 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] 